<!-- TRANSLATORS: %%TOKEN%% placeholders are MX Updater UI labels. Keep them exactly as written. -->

<!-- TOKEN REFERENCE (for generate-help-in and build-help-html):
     UI terms marked with data-token become %%TOKEN%% in help.html.in and
     are replaced with the translated UI label by build-help-html.
     Proper names (Nala, Synaptic, MX Package Installer, MX Repo Manager,
     MX Tools, System Menu) are NOT tokenized.
  FULL                  -> "full"
  BASIC                 -> "basic"
  REFRESH               -> "refresh"
  AUTOMATIC             -> "automatic"
  VIEW_AND_UPGRADE      -> "View and Upgrade"
  CHECK_FOR_UPDATES     -> "Check for Updates"
  HISTORY               -> "History"
  AUTO_UPDATE_LOGS      -> "Auto-update log(s)"
  AUTO_UPDATE_DPKG_LOGS -> "Auto-update dpkg log(s)"
  HELP                  -> "Help"
  PREFERENCES           -> "Preferences"
  ABOUT                 -> "About"
  RESTART_MX_UPDATER    -> "Restart MX Updater"
  HIDE_WHEN_NO_UPDATES  -> "hide when no updates"
  QUIT                  -> "Quit"
  BASIC_UPGRADE         -> "basic upgrade"
  TRANSPARENT_WIREFRAME -> "transparent black/white wireframe"
  TERMINAL_SIZE         -> "terminal size"
  POSITION              -> "position"
  SKIP_UPGRADE_CONFIRMATION -> "skip upgrade confirmation"
  DESKTOP_NOTIFICATIONS -> "desktop notifications"
  AUTOSTART_DELAY       -> "autostart delay"
-->

<h1>MX Updater Help</h1>

<p>MX Updater monitors your MX Linux system for available package updates and
shows a tray icon reflecting the current status. Desktop notifications can also
be shown when updates become available.</p>

<p>Personal preferences can be adjusted by opening <b>MX Updater Preferences</b>
from MX Tools, the System Menu, or the right-click menu of the tray icon.</p>

<div style="text-align: center; line-height: 1;"><img class="screenshot" src="img/settings-full.jpg" alt="Settings window" style="display: block; margin: 0.5em 0 0 0;" /></div>
<h2 style="margin-top: 0.6em;">Upgrade Mode</h2>

<p>Controls the upgrade method and schedule:</p>
<ul>
  <li>%%FULL%% (recommended) -- upgrades packages and allows installing or
      removing packages where needed to satisfy dependencies.</li>
  <li>%%BASIC%% -- upgrades packages but will not install new ones or remove
      existing ones. Safer, but some packages may be kept back.</li>
  <li><b>Nala</b> -- when checked, uses Nala instead of apt as the package manager
      (only shown if Nala is installed).</li>
</ul>
<p>The %%REFRESH%% dropdown sets how often the package list is fetched from the
servers (daily, every 3 days, weekly, every 2 weeks, monthly, or never).
The %%AUTOMATIC%% checkbox enables unattended upgrades on the same schedule.</p>

<h2>Left-Click</h2>

<p>Choose what happens when you left-click the tray icon. Available choices depend
on which applications are installed:</p>
<ul>
  <li>%%VIEW_AND_UPGRADE%% (default) -- opens the MX Updater upgrade window when
      updates are available; falls back to the first installed package manager otherwise.</li>
  <li><b>Synaptic</b> -- opens Synaptic Package Manager (always, with or without updates).</li>
  <li><b>MX Package Installer</b> -- opens MX Package Installer (always, with or without updates).</li>
</ul>
<p>Middle-clicking the tray icon always opens MX Package Installer, regardless
of the left-click setting.</p>

<h3>Right-Click Menu</h3>

<p>Right-clicking the tray icon opens the context menu. Entries are only shown when the
corresponding application is installed or condition applies:</p>
<ul>
  <li>%%VIEW_AND_UPGRADE%% -- open the upgrade window (shown when updates are available).</li>
  <li><b>Synaptic</b> -- open Synaptic Package Manager.</li>
  <li><b>MX Package Installer</b> -- open MX Package Installer.</li>
  <li><b>MX Repo Manager</b> -- open MX Repo Manager.</li>
  <li>%%CHECK_FOR_UPDATES%% -- open a terminal and fetch the latest package lists from the servers.</li>
  <li>%%HISTORY%% -- open MX Updater History to view recently installed or removed packages.</li>
  <li>%%AUTO_UPDATE_LOGS%% -- view the log of automatic upgrades.</li>
  <li>%%AUTO_UPDATE_DPKG_LOGS%% -- view the dpkg log of automatic upgrades.</li>
  <li>%%HELP%% -- this document.</li>
  <li>%%PREFERENCES%% -- open the MX Updater Preferences window.</li>
  <li>%%ABOUT%% -- version information, license and changelog.</li>
  <li>%%RESTART_MX_UPDATER%% -- restart the tray icon process. Useful after manual
      configuration changes; will show a notification if updates are available.</li>
  <li>%%HIDE_WHEN_NO_UPDATES%% -- only shown when the system is up to date; toggles
      whether the tray icon is hidden when there are no updates (not available on KDE).</li>
  <li>%%QUIT%% -- close MX Updater.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Icons</h2>

<p>Choose the icon set used in the system tray. Each set shows two icons: the
left icon is used when updates are available, the right icon when the system
is up to date.</p>
<p>Note: with %%BASIC_UPGRADE%% selected, packages that would require installing
or removing other packages are kept back. When all remaining updates are such
kept-back packages the up-to-date (right) icon is shown.</p>
<p>The %%TRANSPARENT_WIREFRAME%% toggle makes the no-updates icon
transparent with either black or white wire lines. Use black wires on a light
panel, white wires on a dark panel, so the icon does not disappear into the
panel background.</p>
<p>A local administrator can define a custom icon set by placing a configuration
file at <code>/usr/local/etc/mx-updater.conf</code>. An example with full
documentation is provided in
<a href="file:///usr/share/doc/mx-updater/examples/admin-mx-updater.conf">/usr/share/doc/mx-updater/examples/admin-mx-updater.conf</a>.</p>

<h2>Other</h2>

<p>Most entries in this section have a tooltip with further details -- hover over
the label to read it.</p>
<ul>
  <li>%%TERMINAL_SIZE%% -- size of the terminal window that opens during upgrades.
      Options: <i>default</i> (2/3 of screen, capped at 960x600 on wide displays),
      <i>one-third</i>, <i>half</i>, <i>two-thirds</i>, <i>three-quarters</i>,
      <i>nine-tenths</i>, or <i>own</i> (no size hint -- the terminal uses its own
      saved size and position).</li>
  <li>%%POSITION%% -- screen position of the terminal window: center, top-left,
      top-right, bottom-left, or bottom-right. Not available on Wayland or when
      size is set to <i>own</i>.</li>
  <li>%%SKIP_UPGRADE_CONFIRMATION%% -- passes <code>--assume-yes</code> to apt or
      Nala, automatically answering yes to the "Do you want to continue?" prompt.
      Note: configuration questions from individual package scripts (debconf) may
      still appear -- this is normal and not controlled by this setting.
      Use with care: on a yes answer apt will proceed even if the upgrade would
      remove packages to resolve a dependency conflict.</li>
  <li>%%DESKTOP_NOTIFICATIONS%% -- show a desktop notification when updates
      become available.</li>
  <li>%%AUTOSTART_DELAY%% -- wait a short time after login before starting,
      so the desktop is fully set up before MX Updater appears in the tray.</li>
  <li>%%HIDE_WHEN_NO_UPDATES%% -- hide the tray icon when the system is
      up to date. Not available on KDE/Plasma.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Authentication</h2>

<p>Authentication is required to upgrade packages. A password prompt will appear.</p>

<h2>Troubleshooting</h2>

<h3>The tray icon does not appear</h3>
<p>MX Updater starts automatically at login via an XDG autostart entry installed
with the package. If the icon is missing it can be started manually from
<b>MX Tools</b> or the <b>System Menu</b>. On some desktops the system tray
area must be added to the panel manually.</p>

<h3>A password is asked every time I click Upgrade</h3>
<p>This is expected -- authentication is always required for upgrades.</p>

<h3>Updates are not detected</h3>
<p>Use %%CHECK_FOR_UPDATES%% from the right-click menu to force an immediate
check. The terminal window will show any errors, including a locked apt database
from another running package manager.</p>

<h3>The upgrade window shows no packages</h3>
<p>Your system is up to date, or the last check has not completed yet.
Use %%CHECK_FOR_UPDATES%% to refresh.</p>

